## Solution

For this challenge, I chose to explore reference
counting as an alternative garbage collection
technique.

Chapter 26 implements a mark-and-sweep garbage
collector that identifies reachable objects
by tracing references from the VM's roots.

Reference counting takes a different approach.

Instead of waiting until a collection cycle
to identify unused objects, reference counting
tracks how many references point to each object.

When an object's reference count reaches zero,
the object may be reclaimed immediately.

I would explore augmenting the existing clox
collector with reference counting while retaining
mark-and-sweep to handle cyclic references.

The following code demonstrates the main
mechanism and outlines the integration changes
that would be required in clox.

## 1. How Mark-and-Sweep Works

In Chapter 26, clox uses mark-and-sweep.

The collector performs the following steps:

1. Identify roots, including VM stack values,
   global variables, call frames, compiler
   state, and open upvalues.
2. Mark objects reachable from those roots.
3. Traverse references between objects.
4. Remove unreachable interned strings
   from the weak string table.
5. Sweep unreachable objects and reclaim
   their memory.

This algorithm can correctly collect
unreachable object graphs, including cycles.

However, it needs to revisit reachable
objects during garbage collection.

Reference counting offers a different
way to determine when an object is unused.

## 2. Understanding Reference Counting

In reference counting, each object stores
an integer representing the number of
references that currently point to it.

When a new owning reference is created,
the count increases.

When an owning reference is removed,
the count decreases.

If the count reaches zero, the object
has no remaining tracked owners.

Assuming all references are correctly
tracked, the object can be released.

For example:

```text
Create object A:

A.referenceCount = 1

Create another reference to A:

A.referenceCount = 2

Remove one reference:

A.referenceCount = 1

Remove the final reference:

A.referenceCount = 0
```

At this point, the object can be
reclaimed without waiting for a
full mark-and-sweep collection.

The difficult part is ensuring every
owning reference is tracked correctly.

## 3. Add a Reference Count to Obj

The current Obj structure in Chapter 26
contains:

```c
struct sObj {
  ObjType type;
  bool isMarked;
  struct Obj* next;
};
```

For an experimental reference-counted
implementation, I would add a field:

```c
struct sObj {
  ObjType type;
  bool isMarked;
  struct Obj* next;
  size_t referenceCount;
};
```

The new referenceCount field keeps
track of references to the object.

I would also include stddef.h if
it is not already available:

```c
#include <stddef.h>
```

This approach increases the size
of the object header.

It is therefore a separate design
from the packed-header optimization
in Challenge 1.

If both designs were combined,
the reference count would need
its own representation.

## 4. Initialize Reference Counts

In object.c, clox allocates objects
using allocateObject().

An experimental reference-counting
version could initialize the new
field as follows:

```c
static Obj* allocateObject(size_t size,
                           ObjType type) {
  Obj* object = (Obj*)reallocate(NULL, 0, size);

  object->type = type;
  object->isMarked = false;
  object->referenceCount = 0;

  object->next = vm.objects;
  vm.objects = object;

  return object;
}
```

The reference count begins at zero
because no owning reference has been
registered yet.

This is only safe if the allocation
protocol immediately protects the
new object before another garbage
collection can occur.

The original clox implementation
already requires temporary rooting
for objects that are being constructed.

A complete reference-counting system
would need a consistent ownership
protocol for allocation and return
values.

## 5. Increment the Reference Count

I would introduce a helper named
retainObject().

In memory.h:

```c
void retainObject(Obj* object);
```

A basic implementation in memory.c is:

```c
void retainObject(Obj* object) {
  if (object == NULL) return;

  object->referenceCount++;
}
```

Whenever a new owning reference
to an object is created, the VM
would call retainObject().

For example, storing an object
in a variable or a container
may create a new owning reference.

An important detail is that copying
a pointer does not always create
a new owner.

Some references are temporary or
borrowed, so a complete design must
define which references contribute
to the reference count.

## 6. Decrement the Reference Count

The matching operation is releaseObject().

In memory.h:

```c
void releaseObject(Obj* object);
```

The basic idea is:

```c
void releaseObject(Obj* object) {
  if (object == NULL) return;

  if (object->referenceCount == 0) {
    // Ownership bookkeeping error.
    return;
  }

  object->referenceCount--;

  if (object->referenceCount == 0) {
    // No tracked owners remain.
    // Reclaim the object through a
    // reference-count-aware deallocator.
  }
}
```

This illustrates the reference-counting
decision without prematurely freeing
objects.

In a complete implementation,
releasing an object would also
release its owned references.

For example, a closure holds
references to its function and
upvalues.

Those references would need to
be released when the closure
is destroyed.

The object must also be removed
from vm.objects before it is freed.

Otherwise, the existing mark-and-sweep
collector could later try to process
a dangling pointer.

I would not directly call the
existing freeObject() from this
example without first implementing
those ownership and linked-list
requirements.

## 7. Handling Lox Values

clox stores runtime values using
the Value representation.

Not every Value contains a pointer.

Numbers, Boolean values, and nil
do not require reference counting.

Only heap-allocated objects would
participate.

For example:

```c
static void retainValue(Value value) {
  if (IS_OBJ(value)) {
    retainObject(AS_OBJ(value));
  }
}
```

Similarly:

```c
static void releaseValue(Value value) {
  if (IS_OBJ(value)) {
    releaseObject(AS_OBJ(value));
  }
}
```

These helpers would make it easier
to manage reference counts for
objects stored inside Lox values.

However, the entire VM must use
the same ownership convention.

For example, push(), pop(),
variable assignment, global table
updates, and closure creation
would all need careful handling.

## 8. A Standalone Reference-Counting Example

To demonstrate reference counting
without relying on an incomplete
clox integration, I wrote a small
standalone C example.

This example has a simple object
that stores a value and a
reference count.

It is intended as a demonstration
of the reference-counting algorithm.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
  int value;
  size_t referenceCount;
} RCObject;

RCObject* createObject(int value) {
  RCObject* object = malloc(sizeof(RCObject));

  if (object == NULL) {
    return NULL;
  }

  object->value = value;
  object->referenceCount = 1;

  return object;
}

void retain(RCObject* object) {
  if (object != NULL) {
    object->referenceCount++;
  }
}

void release(RCObject* object) {
  if (object == NULL) return;

  if (object->referenceCount == 0) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Invalid reference count.\n");
    abort();
  }

  object->referenceCount--;

  if (object->referenceCount == 0) {
    free(object);
  }
}

int main(void) {
  RCObject* first = createObject(42);

  if (first == NULL) {
    return 1;
  }

  // A second variable shares the same object.
  RCObject* second = first;
  retain(second);

  printf("Value: %d\n", first->value);
  printf("References: %zu\n",
         first->referenceCount);

  // Release the first ownership reference.
  release(first);
  first = NULL;

  printf("References after release: %zu\n",
         second->referenceCount);

  // Release the final ownership reference.
  release(second);
  second = NULL;

  return 0;
}
```

Expected output:

```text
Value: 42
References: 2
References after release: 1
```

The object is allocated with one
owning reference.

When second begins sharing it,
retain() increases the count to two.

Releasing first reduces the count
to one.

Releasing second reduces the count
to zero and frees the object.

The example demonstrates how
reference counting can reclaim
memory without a separate sweep.

It does not implement the full
clox object graph or garbage collector.

## 9. Integrating Reference Counting Into clox

To augment the existing clox
collector, reference tracking
would need to be added wherever
owning references are created,
replaced, or destroyed.

Important areas include:

- VM stack values and temporary roots.
- Global variables and table entries.
- Function constant arrays.
- Closure references to functions.
- Closure references to upvalues.
- Closed upvalues containing objects.
- Compiler-held function objects.
- The lifetime of active call frames.

For example, when a global variable
is assigned a new object, the VM
must keep the new object alive and
release ownership of the old one.

When a closure is freed, the objects
it owns must also have their counts
updated.

Without these changes, reference
counts could become incorrect.

An incorrect count could cause an
object to be freed while it is still
needed, or prevent unused memory
from being reclaimed.

This is why the standalone example
is only a starting point.

## 10. The Problem With Cyclic References

One major limitation of reference
counting is that it cannot reclaim
certain cyclic object graphs.

Consider two objects:

```text
Object A references Object B.

Object B references Object A.
```

Even if the program no longer
holds a reference to either object,
each object still has a reference
from the other.

Conceptually:

```text
A.referenceCount = 1

B.referenceCount = 1
```

Neither count reaches zero.

The objects remain allocated,
even though the program cannot
access them anymore.

This is a memory leak caused
by cyclic references.

A tracing collector such as
mark-and-sweep can reclaim
the objects because neither
is reachable from a root.

## 11. Combining Reference Counting With Mark-and-Sweep

Because reference counting has
difficulty with cycles, I would
retain the Chapter 26
mark-and-sweep collector.

The hybrid approach would use
two complementary mechanisms.

Reference counting could reclaim
objects when their last owning
reference disappears.

Mark-and-sweep would periodically
trace roots to detect remaining
unreachable objects, including
cyclic object graphs.

Both mechanisms would need to
share consistent object metadata
and deallocation logic.

An object immediately freed by
reference counting must first
be removed from the VM's list
of allocated objects.

Likewise, an object reclaimed
during sweeping must release
its owned references without
causing double-free errors.

This coordination adds complexity.

The proposed hybrid design
would therefore require more
changes than simply adding
a referenceCount field.

## 12. Comparing Garbage Collection Algorithms

| Feature | Mark-and-Sweep | Reference Counting |
|---|---|---|
| Basic strategy | Trace roots and free unreachable objects | Count owning references |
| Reclamation | During collection cycles | Potentially when count reaches zero |
| Cyclic garbage | Can collect it | Cannot collect it by simple counting alone |
| Runtime work | Marking and sweeping | Reference-count updates |
| Additional state | Mark bit, object list, gray stack | Reference count and ownership bookkeeping |
| Main concern | Collection pauses | Cycles and reference-management overhead |

Mark-and-sweep focuses on
reachability.

Reference counting focuses
on how many tracked owners
an object has.

Neither approach is automatically
better for every program.

Their costs depend on how
objects are created, referenced,
and discarded.

## 13. Performance Considerations

Reference counting can reclaim
unused objects as soon as their
reference counts reach zero.

This may reduce the need to
wait for a full collection.

However, it also requires
additional bookkeeping
whenever ownership changes.

Programs that frequently
copy or replace references
could spend additional time
updating counts.

Mark-and-sweep avoids those
per-reference updates but
periodically traverses the
reachable object graph and
allocated object list.

A hybrid implementation
would introduce the complexity
of coordinating both systems.

I have not run benchmarks or
integrated reference counting
into clox, so I cannot determine
whether the hybrid approach
would improve performance.

## 14. Testing the Proposed Design

I would begin by testing the
standalone reference-counting
example.

I would verify that objects
are freed only after the final
owning reference is released.

For an integrated clox version,
I would then test:

1. Objects referenced by multiple
   variables.
2. References that are overwritten.
3. Objects captured by closures.
4. Objects held in global variables.
5. Cyclic references between objects.
6. Garbage collection during
   allocation and compilation.

I would also use the
DEBUG_STRESS_GC option
introduced in Chapter 26.

This would help identify
objects being freed too early
or incorrectly retained.

Memory-checking tools could
also help detect invalid
accesses and memory leaks.

These are proposed tests.
They have not been executed.

## 15. Is Reference Counting Worth Using?

I think reference counting
is interesting because its
basic idea is relatively
easy to understand.

An object remains alive while
references to it exist, and
its memory can be reclaimed
when the final owner releases it.

However, applying this idea
to a language interpreter
is more complicated than
the standalone example suggests.

Every owning reference must
be tracked, and cyclic
references require a
separate solution.

I would probably keep
mark-and-sweep as clox's
primary collector unless
testing showed that a
hybrid approach provided
a practical advantage.

The original collector
already handles cyclic
object graphs, which is
important for closures
and more complex objects.
